My app that I built was actually built for a hackathon, but I wanted to develop it further, and so I'm using it for this project. Medi is a medication tracker specifically designed for elderly users.

The UI is extremely simple, and it very efficiently does a couple tasks:
- Tracking your medication
- Making uploading your medication very easy
- Setting reminders that are impossible to ignore or snooze indefinitely

Something that's surviving about mobile app development was how much easier it is to run on iPhone when you build the entire project in Swift.

The other thing that I realized was that setting up the backend with a very basic generic UI and having it spend more time making the things work with the features that you want, rather than having to go at it from a design-first lens, made it much easier to iterate on later, as I was able to design out all the screens manually in Figma and then have it update everything in the app screen, but the functionality was present from the start.

TO RUN:

What you need: a Mac with Xcode 26. To run it on a phone you also need an iPhone on iOS 26, a cable and an Apple Account (a free one works). Nothing needs to be installed.
Get the code: the git clone command, and which Xcode project to open.
Run it in the simulator:
pick the scheme and a simulator, then press Run
tap START and allow alarms
load the demo medications
try a sample-label scan and a test reminder
a note on why the alarm buttons may not work in the simulator
Run it on your iPhone:
trust the computer
set your Team and change the bundle ID
turn on Developer Mode and trust the developer profile
allow the permissions
the free-account limits (the app stops opening after 7 days, and only 3 apps at once)
